# Quran Display Guidelines

These rules reflect Islamic scholarly requirements for properly displaying the Quran in a digital app. The sacred text must be rendered with absolute textual fidelity.

## Bismillah (Basmala)

- Display "بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ" as a **separate decorative header** before the first ayah of every surah
- **Exceptions**: Surah Al-Fatiha (1) includes Bismillah as ayah 1 -- do not duplicate it. Surah At-Tawbah (9) has **no Bismillah** at all
- The Bismillah in Surah An-Naml (27:30) is part of the ayah text, not a header
- Style the Bismillah header distinctly from regular ayah text (centered, decorative)

## Ayah Numbering

- Display ayah numbers clearly alongside each verse
- Use Arabic-Indic numerals (٠١٢٣٤٥٦٧٨٩) or standard numerals -- be consistent
- Ayah numbers should be visually distinct (e.g., circled, colored, or in a badge)

## Surah Header (reader page)

- Show the surah name in both Arabic and English
- Include the total ayah count
- Consider showing: revelation type (Meccan/Medinan), surah number, juz info
- The surah header should be visually prominent and separate from the ayah text

## Surah list cards (Quran tab)

- Use **only** vowelled Arabic surah names from `surah_arabic_vowelled.json` (or fallback `nameArabic`). Do **not** use first-ayah images, CDN images, or any external chapter icons.
- Layout: left = surah number badge + English name (and translation / revelation order when applicable); **center** = Arabic surah name (large, bold); **right** = ayah count (e.g. "7 Ayahs").
- Arabic on the card: `headlineSmall` (or equivalent), `FontWeight.bold`, Quranic font; centered in the card.

## Text Encoding and Fonts

Three Arabic text sources are available:
- **`aya_text`** (KFGQPC JSON): PUA glyphs — only renderable with the bundled `KFGQPCQuranicFontHafsSmart` font. Not parseable for tajweed.
- **`assets/quran/ar/{surahId}.json`**: Standard Uthmanic Arabic (text_uthmani from quran.com API) with **full tashkeel**. Works with any Arabic font and supports tajweed colour analysis. Generated by `tools/build_arabic_and_chapters.dart`.
- **`aya_text_emlaey`** (KFGQPC JSON): Simplified Imla'i script — used for search indexing only. Lacks full diacritics.

**Font options** are managed by `ArabicFontOption` in `settings_controller.dart`. Every face is **bundled** (see `assets/fonts/README.md`); nothing is fetched at runtime. Family names live in `AppFonts` (`lib/theme/app_tokens.dart`) — never hard-code a family string.
- `uthmanic` — bundled KFGQPC Hafs Smart (default, PUA glyphs from `aya_text`)
- `amiri` / `scheherazade` / `notoNaskh` — bundled SIL OFL faces (standard Uthmanic text from `ar/{surahId}.json`)

**Fallback:** the KFGQPC font contains *only* PUA glyphs, so it cannot render standard Uthmanic Unicode. Because the reader switches to standard text whenever tajweed is enabled — which is the default — styles using it declare `AppFonts.arabicFallback` (Amiri). Without that fallback the text silently renders in whatever Arabic font the platform substitutes. `test/arabic_rendering_test.dart` pins this invariant.

When tajweed is **enabled**, always display standard Uthmanic text (`ar/{surahId}.json`) regardless of font — this ensures all fonts can benefit from tajweed colour analysis. When tajweed is **disabled** and the Uthmanic font is active, display `aya_text` (PUA glyphs) for optimal KFGQPC rendering. When tajweed is disabled with a Google Font, use standard Uthmanic text.

## Diacritics and Text Integrity

- **Never strip diacritics** (tashkeel/harakat) from the displayed Arabic text -- they are essential for correct recitation
- Diacritics include: fatha, kasra, damma, sukun, shadda, tanween (fathatain, kasratain, dammatain), maddah
- The Uthmani script must be preserved exactly as sourced -- do not modify or "simplify" the Arabic text
- Use emlaey text only for search indexing; use standard Uthmanic text for non-KFGQPC font display

## Tajweed Coloring

Tajweed colours follow the **Madani mushaf** scheme used by quran.com (github.com/quran/tajweed). Colors are defined as public constants in `lib/ui/widgets/tajweed.dart`.

The engine works with standard Uthmanic Arabic text from `assets/quran/ar/`. When tajweed is enabled, the reader always uses standard Uthmanic text regardless of font selection (so the KFGQPC font also gets tajweed). When tajweed is OFF and KFGQPC font is selected, PUA glyph text is used for optimal rendering.

Currently implemented rules verified against quran.com's `uthmani_tajweed` API for Surah Al-Baqarah 2:1-5:

**Core rules (match Quran.com legend):**
- **Silent letter** (grey #9E9E9E): U+06DF marker, Hamza Wasl (ٱ) non-initial, Lam Shamsiyah, Silent Waw (e.g. الصلوة).
- **Normal madd (2)** (pink #E91E8C): tatweel + superscript alef (ـٰ); lam-alif (لَا).
- **Separated madd (2/4/6)** (orange #FB8C00): maad letter before explicit sukun, end of phrase, or any following letter that is not hamza/shadda (permissible madd).
- **Connected madd (4/5)** (dark pink #D81B60): maad letter before hamza.
- **Necessary madd (6)** (red #F44336): maad letter before shadda; letters with U+0653 (الٓمٓ).
- **Ghunnah / Ikhfa’** (green #43A047): noon/meem with shadda; idgham with ghunnah (receiving letter); ikhfa (noon + receiving letter both green); iqlab (ba); meem ikhfa/idgham.
- **Qalqala (echo)** (cyan #00BCD4): ق ط ب ج د with sukun or at end of ayah (skips if next is same letter with shadda).
- **Tafkhim (heavy)** (dark blue #1565C0): Qaaf (ق) with vowel; Raa (ر) with fatha or damma; seven heavy letters (ص ض ط ظ ق غ خ) when they have a vowel (ط/ق sakin stay qalqala).

**Idgham / silent (two-part):**
- **Idgham with Ghunnah**: noon/tanween+carrier → grey; receiving letter (ي ن م و) → green.
- **Idgham without Ghunnah**: noon/tanween+carrier+receiving letter (ر ل) → all grey.
- **Iqlab**: noon/tanween+carrier → grey; receiving ba → green.

**Silent/assimilated:**
- **U+06DF marker** (grey): letter with ARABIC SMALL HIGH ROUNDED ZERO → silent.
- **Hamza Wasl** (grey): ٱ (U+0671) not at start of ayah.
- **Lam Shamsiyah** (grey): لْ in ال before sun letter with shadda.
- **Silent Waw** (grey): waw + U+0670 where previous has fatha (e.g. الصلوة).

**Key implementation notes:**
- `_hasVowel()` checks for fatha, kasra, damma, all three tanween, and shadda.
- `_hasExplicitSukun()` checks for U+0652 / U+06E1 — used for maad detection (bare letters without marks do NOT count as explicit sukun).
- `_isMaadLetter()` detects alef/waw/ya preceded by corresponding vowel, tatweel with superscript alef (U+0670), and alef-madda (U+0622). Alef-wasla (U+0671) is explicitly excluded.
- Alef-wasla is skipped when checking what follows a maad letter (it's silent in continuous reading).
- Tanween carrier alef/alef-maqsura is greyed out for Idgham/Iqlab detection (matching quran.com).
- Two-part coloring uses an `overrides` map: when processing noon/tanween, the receiving letter index is pre-assigned its colour.
- **Never use WidgetSpan** in tajweed spans — WidgetSpan breaks Arabic cursive letter joining because each inline widget is shaped independently. All spans must be TextSpan so the paragraph-level text shaper can join letters across span boundaries. U+0653 and U+06DF characters are kept in the text as-is and rendered natively by the font.
- 102 unit tests in `test/tajweed_baqarah_test.dart` verify every letter position in 2:1-5 against quran.com's API, plus span type integrity and colour summary counts.

- **Quran Reading & Tajweed guide:** opened from the surah reader app bar (book icon `Icons.menu_book_rounded`) via `showQuranReadingGuideSheet()`. It shows Sajdah, Waqf signs, Tajweed colour legend, and recitation tips; includes a button to open the full Tajweed Guide.
- **Full Tajweed Guide** page (`tajweed_guide_page.dart`) is accessible from Settings, the reader settings sheet, and from within the Quran Reading guide sheet.

## Quran Reading Rules (in-app guide)

The **Quran Reading & Tajweed** guide is opened from the surah reader app bar (book icon, next to the info icon). It is implemented in `lib/ui/widgets/quran_reading_guide_sheet.dart` and must reflect the rules below. Keep this section and the sheet content in sync.

### Sajdah (Prostration) — سجد

- There are **15 sajdah ayahs** in the Quran; they are marked with the symbol ۩ in the text.
- **What to do:** Make one prostration (sujūd): forehead, nose, hands, knees and toes on the ground, facing qibla.
- **What to recite in prostration (Arabic):** سَجَدَ وَجْهِي لِلَّذِي خَلَقَهُ وَشَقَّ سَمْعَهُ وَبَصَرَهُ بِحَوْلِهِ وَقُوَّتِهِ (Sajada wajhī lilladhī khalaqahu wa-shaqqa samʿahu wa-baṣarahu bi-ḥawlihī wa-quwwatihī). **Meaning:** My face prostrated to the One who created it, gave it hearing and sight by His might and power.
- In the reader UI: show a visible sajdah icon/indicator and consider a tooltip that prostration is recommended.

### Waqf (Stop) Signs — أعلام الوقف

If present in the text data, preserve them and explain in the guide:
- **مـ** (Waqf Lazim): mandatory stop — meaning changes if you continue.
- **لا** (La Taqif): do not stop — phrase is incomplete.
- **ج** (Waqf Jaiz): permissible to stop or continue.
- **قلى** (Qeela Alayhil-Waqf): stopping is preferred.
- **صلى** (Qeelal-Waslu): continuing is preferred.
- **س** (Saktah): brief pause without breathing.
- **∴** (Mu'anaqah): stop at one of two marked positions, not both.

### Recitation

- Seek refuge before reciting: Aʿūdhū billāhi minash-shayṭānir-rajīm.
- Begin with Bismillah at the start of each surah (except At-Tawbah).
- Respect stop signs: mandatory stops require a pause; do not stop at "La" (لا) signs.
- Preserve the Quran: avoid contexts where it could be disrespected; do not truncate Arabic mid-word.

## Juz, Hizb, and Ruku Divisions

The data already has a `jozz` (juz) field per ayah:
- **Juz** (30 parts): show the current juz number, especially at boundaries
- **Hizb** (60 parts): each juz has 2 hizb
- **Rub al-Hizb** (240 quarters): marked with ۞ symbol
- **Ruku** (558 sections): thematic paragraph groupings

## Page Numbers

The data has a `page` field per ayah. Consider showing Mushaf page numbers for users who cross-reference with a physical Quran.

## Respectful UX

- Avoid placing Quran text in contexts where it could be disrespected (e.g., overlapping with ads, cut off mid-ayah)
- When truncating for search results, include enough context to preserve meaning
- Use "..." to indicate truncation, never cut mid-word in Arabic
